<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00172</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cbbaf39a-7f4e-4c89-8ebf-1ec40366f4a4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Noah is having trouble solving two equations. In each case, he took steps that he
        thought were acceptable but ended up with statements that are clearly not true.</para>
      <para>Analyze Noah’s work on each equation and the moves he made. Were they acceptable moves?
        Why do you think he ended up with a false equation?</para>
      <para>Discuss your observations with your group and be prepared to share your conclusions. If
        you get stuck, consider solving each equation.</para>
<section>
  <section>
        <title>Example 1</title>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation. \( x+6
        =4x+1-3x \) </para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Apply the Commutative Property. \( x+6 =4x-3x+1 \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Combine like terms. \( x+6 =x+1 \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Subtract
        \(x\) from each side. \( 6 =1 \) </para>
        </section>
        <section>
        <title>Example 2</title>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation. \( 2(5+x)-1=3x+9 \) </para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Apply the
        Distributive Property. \( 10+2x-1 =3x+9 \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Subtract 10 from
        each side.  \( 2x-1 =3x-1 \) </para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Add 1 to each side. \( 2x=3x \) </para>
      </section>

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Why did Noah have no solution for either equation? </para>
          <para>Enter your answer:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> Your answer may vary, but here is an example: All the moves that Noah made were
            acceptable, but because he ended up with a false statement, that means there is no value
            of \(x\) that makes the first equation true. We can see it in the third line:
            \(x+6=x+1\). There is no number that can make the equation true.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Why did Noah arrive at no solution for the equation solved in Example 2?</para>
          <para>Enter your answer:</para>        
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. </para>
          <para>Example 1: All the moves that Noah made were acceptable, but because he ended up
            with a false statement, that means there is no value of \( x \) that makes the first
            equation true. We can see it in the third line: \( x + 6 = x + 1 \). There is no number
            that can make the equation true.</para>
          <para>Example 2: Noah's moves seem acceptable except for the last one. If he had
            subtracted \( 2x \) from each side of the equation, he would have \( 0 = x \). Instead,
            he divided both sides by \( x \) which would give an undefined number if \( x\;\;is\;\;0
            \). </para>
          <para>Noah tried to solve a third equation and ended up with a true statement, without
            variables.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation.</para>
          <para>\( 2 (4 + x) - 2 = 2x + 6 \)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Apply the Distributive Property.</para>
          <para>\( 8+ 2x - 2 =2x + 6 \)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Combine Like Terms.</para>
          <para>\( 2x +6 = 2x +6 \)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Subtract 6 from both sides.       </para>
          <para>\( 2x = 2x \)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Subtract \(2x\) from both sides.</para>
          <para>\( 0=0 \) </para>
          <para>Since there is not a variable and the statement is true, any value of \(x\) would be
            a solution for the original equation. So, this equation has infinitely many solutions.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
      <section>
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>

        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>We cannot divide the number 100 by zero because dividing by zero is undefined.
              Instead, try dividing 100 by 10, then 1, then 0.1, then 0.01. What do you notice happens
              as you divide by smaller numbers? </para>
              <para>Enter your answer here:</para>          
            </problem>
          <solution>
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para>10, 100, 1000, 10,000. For example: The quotients are getting very large.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>Now try dividing the number -100 by 10, by 1, by 0.1, 0.01. What is the same and
              what is different when you compare to question 1?</para>
              <para>Enter your answer here:</para>            
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para>-10, -100, -1000, -10,000. For example: The absolute values of the quotients are the
              same, but the sign is opposite..</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

