                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_09_06_02.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Try to draw a tape diagram that represents 6÷0. Explain why this is so difficult.</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>        
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>The zero takes up no space, so no matter how many copies we have it could never
            reach 6.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Self Check</title>
      <para>Which of the following equations has a solution?</para>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section>
        <title>Equations Without Solutions</title>
        <para>What happens when we solve \( 5z = 5z - 1\)? </para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Subtract \(5z\) to get the \(z\) terms together on one
          side. </para>
        <para>\(\begin{aligned}5z-5z &amp;=5z-5z-1\end{aligned}\) </para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Simplify and the \(z\) terms are gone!. </para>
        <para>\(\begin{aligned}0 &amp;=1\end{aligned}\) </para>
        <para>Since 0 does not equal \(-1\), this equation led to a false statement. There are no
          solutions to this equation.</para>
  
          <section>
          <title>Example:</title>
        <para>Solve \( 5m+3(9+3m)=2(7m−11) \). </para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.
          Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like terms. \(
          5m+27+9m=14m−22 \) \(14m+27=14m−22\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
          equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. \(14m−14m+27=14m−14m−22\)
          \(27=-22\) </para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Check the solution. The equation \(27=−22\) is NOT a true
          statement. The equation 5m+3(9+3m)=2(7m−11) has no solutions. </para>
          </section>
        </section>
  
        <section>
        <title>Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
        <para>What if an equation results in a true statement without variables?</para>
        <para>The equations \( -4 = -4 \), \( 5 = 5 \), and \( 0 = 0 \) are all true statements
          because one side is equal to the other. This means that any value of the variable in the
          original equation would be a solution. When this happens in the final step of an equation,
          there are infinitely many solutions.</para>
          <section>
        <title>Example:</title> 
        <para>Solve \( 2y +6 =2(y+3) \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.
          Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like terms. </para>
        <para>\( 2y +6 =2(y+3) \)</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
          equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. \( 2y−2y+6=2y−2y+6\) \(6=6\) </para>
        <para>\(2y+6=2y+6\)</para>

        <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Check the solution. We say the solution to the equation
          is all of the real numbers.An equation that is true for any value of the variable is called
          an <term class="glossary-tooltip">identity</term>. </para>
        <para>The solution of an identity is all real numbers.</para>
        </section>
  
        <note class="try look-at-me"/>
      <title>Try It: Equations with No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions </title>
          <exercise>
            <problem>
              <para>Solve \( 10x - 4 = 2(5x -2) \)and determine the number of solutions.</para>
  
              <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
                compare your work.</para>
  
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
  
              <para>Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation. </para>
  
              <para>\( 10x - 4 = 2(5x -2) \)</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Distribute </para>
  
              <para>\( 10x - 4 =10x - 4 \)</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
                equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. </para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{aligned}10x - 10x - 4 &amp;=10x - 10x - 4 \\-4 &amp;=-4 \end{aligned}\) </para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check the solution</para>
  
              <para>This is a true statement so there are infinitely many solutions. </para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>

          <exercise>
            <problem>
  
              <para>Solve \( 3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \) and determine the number of solutions.</para>
  
              <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
                compare your work.</para>
  
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
  
              <para>Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation. </para>
  
              <para>\( 3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \)</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Distribute </para>
  
              <para>\( 3x - 4 = 3x -6 \)</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
                equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. </para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{aligned}3x - 3x - 4 &amp;=3x - 3x - 6 \\-4 &amp;=-6 \end{aligned}\) </para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check the solution</para>
  
              <para>The equation \( -4=-6\) is NOT a true statement. The equation \(3x - 4 = 3(x -2)
                \) has no solutions. </para>
  
            </solution>
          </exercise>
 </section>
    </section>
